1
00:00:00,193 --> 00:00:01,633
Rosa needs a refill.

2
00:00:02,895 --> 00:00:07,502
She taps Refill My Prescription in her
pharmacy's mobile app, which triggers an

3
00:00:07,502 --> 00:00:08,261
agentic flow.

4
00:00:09,489 --> 00:00:13,489
That one tap sets off 26 agent and tool
interactions.

5
00:00:14,669 --> 00:00:18,884
Sentry tracks the data through each one and
checks it against company policy.

6
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:22,918
One it marks as risky, let's see why.

7
00:00:24,747 --> 00:00:31,223
Under the hood. The agentic flow is a real
app, running on rossoctl, an agent platform on

8
00:00:31,223 --> 00:00:32,023
Kubernetes.

9
00:00:32,223 --> 00:00:35,726
Five agents and eight tools, each in its own
pod.

10
00:00:35,926 --> 00:00:40,610
The pharmacy assistant takes Rosa's request.
It looks her up, and asks for a clinical

11
00:00:40,610 --> 00:00:43,641
review, which saves the fill to the pharmacy's
records.

12
00:00:43,841 --> 00:00:48,762
Then fulfillment books the courier, and
reports the refill to an outside company: the

13
00:00:48,762 --> 00:00:50,557
wholesaler's demand forecaster.

14
00:00:53,457 --> 00:00:59,244
This is Sentry's console, Data Governance,
recording Rosa's tap as it happens. Sped up.

15
00:01:15,890 --> 00:01:22,226
Each node is an agent, a tool, or the model.
Some tools belong to outside vendors, like the

16
00:01:22,226 --> 00:01:26,683
forecaster. Each arrow is one exchange: a
request and its reply.

17
00:01:26,883 --> 00:01:32,579
The count changes a little from run to run. In
this one, Sentry counted 29 interactions for

18
00:01:32,579 --> 00:01:34,957
the tap, 8 of them calls to the model.

19
00:01:35,157 --> 00:01:38,660
Open any exchange, and Sentry shows who called
whom,

20
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:44,798
and exactly what was sent: here, her name and
birth date, going to the patient lookup.

21
00:01:44,999 --> 00:01:50,076
With the risk layer on, one exchange turns
red: the report to the forecaster.

22
00:01:52,067 --> 00:01:57,166
Sentry is data governance on top of Cortex,
which monitors the traffic between agents and

23
00:01:57,166 --> 00:01:58,426
everything they touch.

24
00:01:59,216 --> 00:02:02,795
It's built on lineage, classification, and
risk assessment.

25
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:10,667
How does Sentry see all this? Cortex runs a
sidecar, a small proxy, beside every agent and

26
00:02:10,667 --> 00:02:13,376
tool. There is no Sentry code in the app.

27
00:02:13,576 --> 00:02:19,105
For every exchange, the sidecar sends Sentry
two records: the request and the response,

28
00:02:19,105 --> 00:02:20,376
with their payloads.

29
00:02:20,576 --> 00:02:23,724
From these, Sentry rebuilds who called whom.

30
00:02:23,924 --> 00:02:29,774
Classification reads every payload and names
each value in it: a person's name, a medical

31
00:02:29,774 --> 00:02:31,220
record number, a date.

32
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:36,424
Lineage follows each value from exchange to
exchange, and its label travels with it.

33
00:02:36,625 --> 00:02:41,551
Risk assessment checks every exchange against
the company policy, and records a verdict.

34
00:02:43,578 --> 00:02:46,086
Let's take one rule from the company policy.

35
00:02:47,025 --> 00:02:49,952
Personal data must not leave the organization.

36
00:02:51,986 --> 00:02:57,467
In this demo, Sentry holds the company policy
as six rules, graded from critical to low.

37
00:02:57,467 --> 00:02:59,709
Three of them matter for this story.

38
00:02:59,909 --> 00:03:06,011
The first rule: personal data, sent to an
untrusted external destination. Its suggested

39
00:03:06,011 --> 00:03:07,484
enforcement is block.

40
00:03:07,684 --> 00:03:12,189
The second says the same for health data, and
the third for restricted data.

41
00:03:12,388 --> 00:03:18,238
Untrusted means outside the organization. The
cluster's own services count as inside. Here

42
00:03:18,238 --> 00:03:21,033
the contracted courier runs as one of them.

43
00:03:21,233 --> 00:03:24,095
So does any address on a list the company
sets.

44
00:03:24,296 --> 00:03:28,797
The forecaster's address is on neither:
external, and untrusted.

45
00:03:28,997 --> 00:03:34,503
Sentry evaluates the rules with OPA, the Open
Policy Agent. Here are the first rule's

46
00:03:34,503 --> 00:03:36,641
conditions, as Sentry shows them.

47
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:41,923
Any flow that carries personal data to a
destination that is external and untrusted

48
00:03:41,923 --> 00:03:42,842
fires the rule.

49
00:03:44,877 --> 00:03:45,884
Level one.

50
00:03:47,110 --> 00:03:50,046
In Rosa's record, the context is all there.

51
00:03:50,587 --> 00:03:53,734
Her name and beside it her medical record
number.

52
00:03:54,861 --> 00:03:59,868
Both are personal data and the vendor is
external outside the organization.

53
00:04:00,676 --> 00:04:04,752
Sending them there breaks the policy, so
Sentry recommends blocking it.

54
00:04:05,589 --> 00:04:09,805
With full context at the point of decision,
this is local enforcement.

55
00:04:10,478 --> 00:04:13,907
Most data governance tools can do it, and so
does Sentry.

56
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:21,484
Here is what actually happens inside Sentry,
at level one: the report to the forecaster,

57
00:04:21,484 --> 00:04:22,729
from Rosa's refill.

58
00:04:22,929 --> 00:04:27,138
Its payload holds her name, her birth date,
and her medical record number.

59
00:04:27,338 --> 00:04:33,403
Classification reads it: it finds each value,
names its type, and gives it its tags and its

60
00:04:33,403 --> 00:04:34,202
sensitivity.

61
00:04:34,403 --> 00:04:39,826
Her name is found right here: personal data,
restricted. A check that sees only this

62
00:04:39,826 --> 00:04:41,053
message has enough.

63
00:04:41,252 --> 00:04:47,230
So the input Sentry sends to OPA reads:
personal data, restricted, going to an

64
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:49,682
external, untrusted destination.

65
00:04:49,883 --> 00:04:52,743
The first and third rules fire on her name.

66
00:04:52,942 --> 00:04:58,975
OPA combines the rules, most restrictive
first: risk level critical, suggested

67
00:04:58,975 --> 00:05:00,445
enforcement: block.

68
00:05:00,645 --> 00:05:03,566
Sentry raises an alert and recommends the
block.

69
00:05:03,766 --> 00:05:09,289
The courier gets her name and address too. It
runs inside the organization, so that call is

70
00:05:09,289 --> 00:05:09,775
allowed.

71
00:05:11,781 --> 00:05:14,068
But what happens when the context is gone?

72
00:05:15,397 --> 00:05:18,688
Level two. The app now sends only the number.

73
00:05:19,577 --> 00:05:25,387
At release, it's out of context, so given only
the local context, it's classified as a

74
00:05:25,387 --> 00:05:26,941
generic operational ID.

75
00:05:28,232 --> 00:05:30,314
Local enforcement would let it through.

76
00:05:30,898 --> 00:05:34,040
But Sentry's lineage carries the context with
the data.

77
00:05:34,638 --> 00:05:38,359
Four hops back, that number was Rosa's medical
record number.

78
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:44,997
It's still personal data, so sending it breaks
the policy, and Sentry recommends blocking it.

79
00:05:46,195 --> 00:05:49,055
With full context, Sentry recommends a block.

80
00:05:49,623 --> 00:05:53,912
Four hops downstream and out of context, it
still catches the leak.

81
00:05:55,947 --> 00:06:01,574
Here is what actually happens inside Sentry,
at level two. The report now sends only the

82
00:06:01,574 --> 00:06:02,021
number.

83
00:06:02,221 --> 00:06:08,481
Read alone, the classifier calls it an
operational ID: internal. No rule fires on

84
00:06:08,481 --> 00:06:08,868
that.

85
00:06:09,068 --> 00:06:13,738
Now track that number on the graph. Every
exchange that carried it lights up, the

86
00:06:13,738 --> 00:06:14,718
model's included.

87
00:06:14,917 --> 00:06:20,354
Hop one. The patient lookup answers the
assistant: her name and her medical record

88
00:06:20,354 --> 00:06:23,272
number, together. Here the label is created.

89
00:06:23,472 --> 00:06:27,119
Hop two. The assistant passes the number to
fulfillment.

90
00:06:27,319 --> 00:06:30,819
Hop three. Fulfillment passes it to the report
tool.

91
00:06:31,119 --> 00:06:34,408
Hop four. The report sends it to the
forecaster.

92
00:06:34,919 --> 00:06:40,633
At every hop, Sentry matches the exact value,
so the label, medical record number, travels

93
00:06:40,633 --> 00:06:41,141
with it.

94
00:06:41,341 --> 00:06:48,087
OPA now gets the carried label. Three rules
fire. The decision: risk level critical,

95
00:06:48,087 --> 00:06:50,416
suggested enforcement: block.

96
00:06:52,337 --> 00:06:55,413
But what if the leak comes from a different
session altogether?

97
00:06:56,503 --> 00:07:01,646
A nightly job pulls the number from a shared
database, with no name and no context.

98
00:07:02,865 --> 00:07:08,074
Sentry's cross-session lineage carries the
full context across sessions, so this leak is

99
00:07:08,074 --> 00:07:08,725
caught too.

100
00:07:10,774 --> 00:07:16,079
Here is what actually happens inside Sentry,
at level three. The nightly job runs as a

101
00:07:16,079 --> 00:07:18,485
session of its own, with its own trace.

102
00:07:18,685 --> 00:07:23,956
The shared database is the pharmacy's records
service. During Rosa's refill, it saved the

103
00:07:23,956 --> 00:07:25,259
fill under her number.

104
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:30,904
At night, the batch reads the day's fills from
the records. Read alone, the number in that

105
00:07:30,904 --> 00:07:32,960
answer is an operational ID again.

106
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:37,828
Lineage reaches back across sessions: the
records got that number in Rosa's refill,

107
00:07:37,828 --> 00:07:42,383
where it came from the patient lookup, next to
her name. The label comes with it.

108
00:07:42,583 --> 00:07:48,500
Reading the records stays inside, so that call
is allowed. The risk is the next call: the

109
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:53,686
batch's export to the forecaster. The
decision: risk level critical, suggested

110
00:07:53,686 --> 00:07:56,943
enforcement: block, linked back to Rosa's
refill.

111
00:07:58,959 --> 00:08:02,177
Sentry doesn't fix the app, it applies the
policy.

112
00:08:03,371 --> 00:08:08,887
Once the app is fixed so it no longer releases
personal data, sending the vendor only what it

113
00:08:08,887 --> 00:08:10,666
needs, Sentry lets it through.

114
00:08:12,446 --> 00:08:18,740
Inside Sentry, the fixed app. The report now
sends the drug and the dates. No patient key.

115
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:24,376
Nothing in it is personal data, so no rule
fires: allowed, no alert.

116
00:08:24,576 --> 00:08:27,796
The nightly batch is fixed the same way.
Allowed too.

117
00:08:27,997 --> 00:08:32,576
The fix is in the app. Sentry found the leak,
and it confirms the fix.

118
00:08:34,524 --> 00:08:39,834
This way, Rosa gets her refill, and the policy
holds, keeping the company in line with data

119
00:08:39,834 --> 00:08:41,176
protection regulations.

120
00:08:42,289 --> 00:08:44,508
Local enforcement sees one message.

121
00:08:45,514 --> 00:08:51,317
Sentry sees its whole history, protecting data
locally, within a session and across session

122
00:08:51,317 --> 00:08:52,018
boundaries.

123
00:08:53,864 --> 00:09:00,461
Every verdict in this film is Sentry's own,
from one run on Kubernetes: 5 sessions, 105

124
00:09:00,461 --> 00:09:02,584
exchanges judged, 3 flagged.

125
00:09:02,784 --> 00:09:07,902
Sentry watches every app in the cluster, and
its risk dashboard shows the whole picture.

126
00:09:07,902 --> 00:09:12,148
Here is the pharmacy, beside a travel booking
app, under the same policy.

127
00:09:12,348 --> 00:09:20,024
Of 254 exchanges, 3 are critical. 12 are high,
11 medium and 2 low: card and financial data

128
00:09:20,024 --> 00:09:25,423
moving inside the travel app. Worth reviewing,
not an emergency.

129
00:09:25,623 --> 00:09:27,912
These are the rules behind those numbers.

130
00:09:29,623 --> 00:09:34,540
At the top: the sessions that sent Rosa's
number to the forecaster, the three we just

131
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:35,060
followed.

132
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:39,037
Below them, lower-severity traces, listed for
follow-up.

133
00:09:40,822 --> 00:09:44,753
Sentry, with lineage and risk, is live in the
next release.
